 @buckiesmalls  I would agree if we had a proper storage for all of the nuclear waste for the next thousands of years. But you have to consider that a) this is not the case (in germany at least) and these nuclear reactors are expenive af (building, maintanence and especially deconstruction) so it's more logical to invest in solar arrays, dams and wind mills.

And regardig batteries: Extremely expensive in huge quantities and the lithium/cobalt mining (yes I know you can use LixMn2O4 or LiFePO4 as cathodes too but the energy density is lower compared to cobalt-oxide), as well as the production of them isn't the most eco-friendly either. I think it would be better to work together with different countrys to balance fluctuations and use hydrogen and fuel cells as a means for long-ish term power storage. But the problem there would be that you loose energy by storing it via hydrogen fuel cells and the catalysts for them are also not cheap (Platinum, Rhodium, and so on)

So in a nutshell its not only the fossil companies and the oil-lobby who tell everyone that "green"-energy is bad, there are actual reasons behind that. But in general you are right, coal and oil (especially coal because of the NOx, SOx and the CO2) is waaaaay worse than wind and solar (nuclear fission is debatable).

